            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your house is reasonably air tight, with the exception of a few main locations that will become more air tight if you implement the other upgrade recommendations in this report:



** An upgrade to a "direct vent", high efficiency furnace &amp; hot water heater will eliminate the need for the current chimney &amp; one of the two combustion air supply pipes - reducing air leakage heat loss through both "pipes"



** Arranging for a "direct connection" of the second combustion air supply to the wood stove, rather than just having a 6" open pipe sitting on the floor of the basement.



** The basement windows are older and allow a fair bit of air leakage



Savings: I have modeled a modest 10% reduction in overall air leakage that could potentially save $ 60 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1.8 GJ, and qualify for a rebate through the Canada Greener Homes Program. Also read the Ventilation Upgrade sections of this report.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $715 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on achieving the 10% reduction I have modeled.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <windows>

You recently upgraded many of the windows on the main floor of your house. There are 6 older wood windows in the basement that you may still choose to upgrade. If you proceed, I recommend you install ENERGY STAR?????? qualified, full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or fixed-frame windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.



Savings:

By replacing these 6 windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 91 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,950 in rebates through the Canada Greener Homes Program.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 or $325.00 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>

During my assessment, your house may appear under-ventilated at certain times of the year. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality



You can either operate a kitchen range hood of bathroom fan a little more or perhaps consider having an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI. I recommend you install one that is a minimum 75% efficient and has an ECM circulation motor to keep operation costs low.



NOTE: As you eliminate the 2 x Combustion Air supply pipes (in conjunction with the Heating System upgrade recommendation), perhaps your heating contractor can convert the one combustion air supply pipe (in the mechanical room) to a fresh air "makeup" supply ...... directly connecting it to the return air plenum of the furnace and incorporating a manual damper that you can adjust from "fully closed" to "fully open". If the indoor air is stale, stuffy and too humid, than gradually open the damper. If the indoor air is too dry &amp; feels cool each time the furnace starts up, than gradually close the damper ......... until you find the sweet spot for your home.



Savings:

By installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV, this will increase the ventilation of your house, with an estimated $ 97 increase per year in energy costs, that will mostly be offset if you can address the air leakage locations outlined previously in this report. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 151 litre propane hot water tank, that is co-vented with the furnace up a chimney, and it operates at about a 0.54 Energy Factor (EF). This hot water tank has a continuous pilot light that consumes propane all year long.



I have modeled an option for you to consider replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an Energy Factor of 0.95 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.95 EF, could potentially save $ 194 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an 80% efficient propane furnace, that is co-vented with the hot water tank up a chimney. There is a 6" combustion air supply pipe in the mechanical room (closet) that provides the combustion air required for these 2 appliances. There is also another 6" combustion air supply pipe that provides combustion air to the wood stove - you should investigate to see if a "direct connection" of this second combustion air supply to the wood stove is possible, rather than just having a 6" open pipe sitting on the floor of the basement.



I have modeled an option for you to upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 96% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 96% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 543 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 14.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil- or gas-fired furnace or boiler with an ENERGY STAR certified natural gas- or propane-fired furnace or boiler that is a minimum 97% AFUE. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



????????? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

????????? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

????????? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

????????? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

????????? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

????????? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Hay River, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment that vents to the outside (for your house, the range hood, clothes dryer &amp; the HRV that has had it's fresh air supply disconnected), indicated a pressure drop of 2.8 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace, hot water tank &amp; wood stove are all chimney vented and are susceptible to "back drafting".



During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke &amp; carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood (a recirculating model that does not vent outside)

        - 1 bathroom fan

        - The clothes dryer



NOTE: As you eliminate the 2 x Combustion Air supply pipes (in conjunction with the Heating System upgrade recommendation), perhaps your heating contractor can convert the one combustion air supply pipe (in the mechanical room) to a fresh air "makeup" supply ...... directly connecting it to the return air plenum of the furnace and incorporating a manual damper that you can adjust from "fully closed" to "fully open". If the indoor air is stale, stuffy and too humid, than gradually open the damper. If the indoor air is too dry &amp; feels cool each time the furnace starts up, than gradually close the damper ......... until you find the sweet spot for your home.



If you decide to replace the bathroom fan, please choose an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified units. For the new bathroom fans, you may want to consider installing a timer switch (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Dec. 1, 2021 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.33 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.36 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.14 per litre 

Propane - $0.86 per litre delivered, $3.31 per m3 piped to the house

Wood - $341.25 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.20 per bag, $268.12 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: ken.baigent@aea.nt.ca

Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!



